// CARD a2-01 · SEE: a real globe turning in close-up; on "sold" gold lines shoot out from Denmark to new places one after another while a counter runs to 42 · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@map_burst;map:stock_world_globe_02;x:.5;y:.45;n:42;go:sold;lab:COUNTRIES BY 1966;in:right"
{excited} The system took off. By nineteen sixty-six, LEGO was sold in forty-two **countries**.

// CARD a2-02 · SEE: the real LEGOLAND Billund of the 1970s: a brick-built train full of passengers, the camera travelling with it · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@reel;under:lego_legoland_1977_09;ua:0;move:right;z:.4;out:right"
In nineteen sixty-eight, so many visitors turned up at the factory that LEGO built them a park, **LEGOLAND**.

// CARD a2-03 · SEE: the real park seen from its tower; on "drew" tiny walkers pour onto its paths in waves while the count runs past a dashed ESTIMATE mark and locks on 625,000 · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@crowd_fill;pic:lego_legoland_tower_view;n:900;go:drew;to:625000;est:312500;lab:VISITORS · FIRST SEASON 1968;sub:the estimate · about half;in:right"
The first season drew six hundred and twenty-five thousand people, about twice the **estimate**.

// CARD a2-04 · SEE: the camera pushes hard into a real minifigure's smiling yellow face until it fills the frame · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@reel;under:lego_minifig_head_macro;ua:0;move:in;z:.1;out:right"
Then in nineteen seventy-eight came the **minifigure**.

// CARD a2-05 · SEE: the real Miniland of 1977, empty streets of red roofs; on "years" chalk tally marks scratch across it in fives, 46 of them; NOBODY LIVED HERE sets under · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@years_tally;pic:lego_legoland_1977;n:46;go:years;span:1.8;lab:46 YEARS · NOBODY IN THE SETS;in:right"
For forty-six years, LEGO's worlds had had no people in **them**. (J)

// CARD a2-06 · SEE: Kjeld Kirk Kristiansen at a lectern in 1996, the camera travelling to his face; on "Kjeld" a name card slides in at the top · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@name_strap;pic:lego_kjeld_1996_award;go:Kjeld;name:KJELD KIRK KRISTIANSEN;role:the founder's grandson · CEO 1979–2004;fx:.6;fy:.35;out:right"
In nineteen seventy-nine, the founder's grandson, Kjeld Kirk Kristiansen, took **charge**.

// CARD a2-07 · SEE: real LEGO product photos dealt onto a dark table faster and faster; on "parks", "Clothes", "Watches", "games" one card lands big in the middle, its footage playing inside · SEAM in right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@catalogue_fan;cards:lego_city_space_shuttle~lego_technic_tractor~lego_city_train_toronto~lego_duplo_large~lego_minifig_skateboard~lego_legoland_logflume~lego_technic_supra_build~lego_minifig_offroad~lego_legoland_castle~lego_model_red_house_theater;go:nineties;keys:lego_clip_roller_coaster@parks@PARKS~lego_clip_clothing_store@Clothes@CLOTHES~lego_clip_watch_face@Watches@WATCHES~lego_clip_arcade_family@games@VIDEO GAMES;in:right"
And by the nineties, LEGO was saying yes to **everything**.
More parks. Clothes. Watches. Video **games**. (J)

// CARD a2-08 · SEE: the real LEGO online shop of March 2004 in a browser window, scrolling down through its themes; on "worlds" the headline is highlighted · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@web_scroll;doc:lego_doc_wayback_shop_2004_03_26;crop:0,.41;from:0;to:.22;mark:.11,.155;go:worlds;url:shop.lego.com · archived 26 March 2004;out:right"
New themes, new worlds, new pieces every **year**.

// CARD a2-09 · SEE: one real part photo fills the frame; on "Designers" it splits into 2, 4, 8 … 64 tiles of different parts while a counter runs 6,000 → 12,000+ · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@tile_multiply;tiles:lego_brick_2x4_stud~lego_technic_gear~lego_minifig_legs~lego_technic_bits~lego_duplo_old~lego_technic_gears~lego_brick_color_pile~lego_patent_brick~lego_minifig_head_macro~lego_bricks_assorted;go:Designers;steps:6;from:6000;to:12000;suf:+;lab:DIFFERENT PARTS;in:right"
Designers went from about six thousand different parts to more than twelve **thousand**.

// CARD a2-10 · SEE: a hundred bricks in a grid over real sorted Technic parts; on "ninety" ninety of them get a red 1 badge and sink, one after another; the ten left light up gold · SEAM out right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@hundred_parts;bg:lego_technic_bits;pct:90;go:ninety;lab:90 IN 100 · USED IN ONE DESIGN;sub:case literature · Robertson and Breen;out:right"
And about ninety percent of the components were used in just one **design**.

// CARD a2-11 · MOTIF · SEE: a warehouse buried under bricks and boxes (AI moment art); the brick drops and lands on the heap, one more among thousands; A CATALOGUE sets above · SEAM in right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@brick_drop;bg:lego_scene_brick_warehouse_chaos;go:catalogue;x:.42;y:.6;h:.11;lab:A CATALOGUE;lx:.5;z:.3;move:right;in:right"
{wry} A system where nothing gets reused isn't a system. It's a **catalogue**. (J)

// CARD a2-12 · SEE: a steel mould in front of a working factory; on "mould" a new cavity is cut into it in a shower of sparks and $10,000s is scratched beside it · SEAM out right
# cine act=doubt fx="code:@mould_mill;clip:lego_clip_factory_metal;go:mould;price:$10,000s;lab:ONE NEW SHAPE · ONE NEW MOULD;out:right"
Every new shape needs its own steel **mould**, and a mould can cost tens of thousands of dollars.

// CARD a2-13 · SEE: an accounts ledger slides along under the lamp, 1993 to 1997 ticked in black; on "loss" 1998 turns red and a red LOSS stamp hits its row · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@ledger_red;y0:1993;y1:1998;go:loss;word:LOSS;lab:THE YEAR'S RESULT;in:right"
In nineteen ninety-eight, LEGO posted a **loss**.

// CARD a2-14 · SEE: a giant brick-built Millennium Falcon at an exhibition, the camera pushing along it; 1999 · FIRST OUTSIDE LICENCE sets above · SEAM out right
# cine act=rise fx="code:@reel;under:lego_starwars_millennium_falcon;ua:0;move:in;z:.35;txt:1999 · FIRST OUTSIDE LICENCE;tat:signed;ty:.16;out:right"
In nineteen ninety-nine it signed its first outside licence: Star **Wars**.

// CARD a2-15 · SEE: two prints on the desk: a brick-built AT-AT and LEGO's own 2002 homepage with Galidor; on "money" gold coins pour onto the AT-AT; on "not" the Galidor print drains grey and sags · SEAM in right
# cine act=fall fx="code:@coin_stream;a:lego_starwars_atat;b:lego_doc_wayback_home_2002_crop;alab:STAR WARS · LICENSED;blab:GALIDOR · LEGO'S OWN · 2002;go:money;dry:not;in:right"
Star Wars made money. But many of LEGO's own new worlds did **not**.
